" (https://aclanthology.org/2023.acl-long.235.pdf)\nciteturn57view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 26; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn53view4\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 1322\nL0@P0: Proceedings of the 61st Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics\nL1@P0: Volume 1: Long Papers, pages 4277–4302\nL2@P0: July 9-14, 2023 ©2023 Association for Computational Linguistics\nL3@P0: MASSIVE: A 1M-Example Multilingual Natural Language Understanding\nL4@P0: Dataset with 51 Typologically-Diverse Languages\nL5@P0: Jack FitzGerald ∗1 Christopher Hench1 Charith Peris1 Scott Mackie1\nL6@P0: Kay Rottmann1 Ana Sanchez1 Aaron Nash1 Liam Urbach1\nL7@P0: Vishesh Kakarala1 Richa Singh1 Swetha Ranganath2 Laurie Crist3\nL8@P0: Misha Britan1 Wouter Leeuwis1 Gokhan Tur1 Prem Natarajan4\nL9@P0: 1Amazon 2Microsoft 3Tripadvisor 4Capital One\nL10@P0: Abstract\nL11@P0: We present the MASSIVE dataset—\nL12@P0: Multilingual Amazon Slu resource package\nL13@P0: (SLURP) for Slot-filling, Intent classification,\nL14@P0: and Virtual assistant Evaluation. MASSIVE\nL15@P0: contains 1M realistic, parallel, labeled virtual\nL16@P0: assistant utterances spanning 51 languages, 18\nL17@P0: domains, 60 intents, and 55 slots. MASSIVE\nL18@P0: was created by tasking professional translators\nL19@P0: to localize the English-only SLURP dataset\nL20@P0: into 50 typologically diverse languages from\nL21@P0: 29 genera. We also present modeling results\nL22@P0: on XLM-R and mT5, including exact match\nL23@P0: accuracy, intent classification accuracy, and\nL24@P0: slot-filling F1 score. We have released our\nL25@P0: dataset, modeling code, and models publicly.\nL26@P0: 1 Introduction and Description\nL27@P0: Natural Language Understanding (NLU) is a\nL28@P0: machine’s ability to understand the meaning and\nL29@P0: relevant entities from text. For instance, given the\nL30@P0: utterance what is the temperature in\nL31@P0: new york, an NLU model might classify the\nL32@P0: intent as weather_query and fill the slots as\nL33@P0: weather_descriptor: temperature\nL34@P0: and place_name: new york. Our partic\u0002ular focus of NLU is one component of Spoken\nL35@P0: Language Understanding (SLU), in which raw\nL36@P0: audio is first converted to text before NLU is\nL37@P0: performed (Young, 2002; Wang et al., 2005;\nL38@P0: Tur and Mori, 2011). SLU is the foundation of\nL39@P0: voice-based virtual assistants like Alexa, Siri,\nL40@P0: and Google Assistant. Though virtual assistants\nL41@P0: have advanced incredibly in the past decade, they\nL42@P0: still only support a small fraction of the world’s\nL43@P0: 7,000+ languages (Simons, 2022). Challenges\nL44@P0: *Corresponding author, jgmf@amazon.com. All au\u0002thors were associated with Amazon at the time of publication.\nL45@P0: for multilingualism span the software stack and\nL46@P0: a variety of operational considerations, but one\nL47@P0: difficulty in creating massively multilingual NLU\nL48@P0: models is the lack of labeled data for training and\nL49@P0: evaluation, particularly data that is realistic for the\nL50@P0: task and that is natural for each given language.\nL51@P0: High naturalness typically requires human-based\nL52@P0: vetting, which is often costly.\nL53@P0: We present MASSIVE (Multilingual Amazon\nL54@P0: SLU Resource Package (SLURP) for Slot fill\u0002ing, Intent classification, and Virtual assistant\nL55@P0: Evaluation), a new 1M-example dataset composed\nL56@P0: of realistic, human-created virtual assistant utter\u0002ance text spanning 51 languages, 60 intents, 55\nL57@P0: slot types, and 18 domains. With the English seed\nL58@P0: data included, there are 587k train utterances, 104k\nL59@P0: dev utterances, 152k test utterances, and 153k ut\u0002terances currently held out for the MMNLU-22\nL60@P0: competition, which will be released after the com\u0002petition. We have released our data, code, and\nL61@P0: models 1.\nL62@P0: MASSIVE was created by localizing the SLURP\nL63@P0: NLU dataset (created only in English) in a parallel\nL64@P0: manner. SLURP is described further in Section 2,\nL65@P0: linguistic analyses of the dataset in Section 3, and\nL66@P0: the localization process in Section 4.3. Results for\nL67@P0: Massively Multilingual NLU (MMNLU) modeling,\nL68@P0: in which a single model can perform NLU on any\nL69@P0: of the incoming languages, are given in Section 5.\nL70@P0: 2 Related Work\nL71@P0: Prior researchers have emphasized the need to ex\u0002plore the unique challenges of low-resource lan\u0002guages (Simpson et al., 2008; Strassel and Tracey,\nL72@P0: 2016; Cruz and Cheng, 2020; Lakew et al., 2020;\nL73@P0: Marivate et al., 2020; Magueresse et al., 2020;\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.usenix.org/system/files/osdi24-agrawal.pdf)\nciteturn57view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 19; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn49view1\",\"lineno\":10}); Total lines: 1576\nL0@P0: This paper is included in the Proceedings of the\nL1@P0: 18th USENIX Symposium on Operating Systems\nL2@P0: Design and Implementation.\nL3@P0: July 10–12, 2024 • Santa Clara, CA, USA\nL4@P0: 978-1-939133-40-3\nL5@P0: Open access to the Proceedings of the\nL6@P0: 18th USENIX Symposium on Operating\nL7@P0: Systems Design and Implementation\nL8@P0: is sponsored by\nL9@P0: Taming Throughput-Latency Tradeoff\nL10@P0: in LLM Inference with Sarathi-Serve\nL11@P0: Amey Agrawal, Georgia Institute of Technology; Nitin Kedia, Ashish Panwar,\nL12@P0: Jayashree Mohan, Nipun Kwatra, and Bhargav Gulavani, Microsoft Research India;\nL13@P0: Alexey Tumanov, Georgia Institute of Technology; Ramachandran Ramjee,\nL14@P0: Microsoft Research India\nL15@P0-1: https://www.usenix.org/conference/osdi24/presentation/agrawalTaming Throughput-Latency Tradeoff in LLM Inference with Sarathi-Serve\nL16@P1: Amey Agrawal*2\nL17@P1: , Nitin Kedia1, Ashish Panwar1, Jayashree Mohan1, Nipun Kwatra1,\nL18@P1: Bhargav S. Gulavani1, Alexey Tumanov2, and Ramachandran Ramjee1\nL19@P1: 1Microsoft Research India\nL20@P1: 2Georgia Institute of Technology\nL21@P1: Abstract\nL22@P1: Each LLM serving request goes through two phases. The\nL23@P1: first is prefill which processes the entire input prompt and pro\u0002duces the first output token and the second is decode which\nL24@P1: generates the rest of output tokens, one-at-a-time. Prefill it\u0002erations have high latency but saturate GPU compute due to\nL25@P1: parallel processing of the input prompt. In contrast, decode\nL26@P1: iterations have low latency but also low compute utilization\nL27@P1: because a decode iteration processes only a single token per\nL28@P1: request. This makes batching highly effective for decodes\nL29@P1: and consequently for overall throughput. However, batching\nL30@P1: multiple requests leads to an interleaving of prefill and decode\nL31@P1: iterations which makes it challenging to achieve both high\nL32@P1: throughput and low latency.\nL33@P1: We introduce an efficient LLM inference scheduler, Sarathi\u0002Serve, to address this throughput-latency tradeoff. Sarathi\u0002Serve introduces chunked-prefills which splits a prefill request\nL34@P1: into near equal sized chunks and creates stall-free schedules\nL35@P1: that adds new requests in a batch without pausing ongoing\nL36@P1: decodes. Stall-free scheduling unlocks the opportunity to im\u0002prove throughput with large batch sizes while minimizing the\nL37@P1: effect of batching on latency. Furthermore, uniform batches\nL38@P1: in Sarathi-Serve ameliorate the imbalance between iterations,\nL39@P1: resulting in minimal pipeline bubbles.\nL40@P1: Our techniques yield significant improvements in inference\nL41@P1: performance across models and hardware under tail latency\nL42@P1: constraints. For Mistral-7B on single A100 GPUs, we achieve\nL43@P1: 2.6× higher serving capacity and up to 3.7× higher serving ca\u0002pacity for the Yi-34B model on two A100 GPUs as compared\nL44@P1: to vLLM. When used with pipeline parallelism on Falcon\u0002180B, Sarathi-Serve provides up to 5.6× gain in the end-to\u0002end serving capacity. The source code for Sarathi-Serve is\nL45@P1: available at https://github.com/microsoft/sarathi-serve.\nL46@P1: *Part of this work was done during an internship at MSR India.\nL47@P1: 0 100 200 300\nL48@P1: Time (s)\nL49@P1: 0K\nL50@P1: 10K\nL51@P1: 20K\nL52@P1: 30K\nL53@P1: Tokens Generated\nL54@P1: 200 210 220\nL55@P1: Generation stall\nL56@P1: Sarathi-Serve\nL57@P1: vLLM\nL58@P1: (a) Generation stall.\nL59@P1: 0.55 0.7 1.0\nL60@P1: Queries per Second\nL61@P1: 0.00\nL62@P1: 0.25\nL63@P1: 0.50\nL64@P1: 0.75\nL65@P1: 1.00\nL66@P1: 1.25\nL67@P1: Time-between-tokens\nL68@P1: (P99, seconds)\nL69@P1: vLLM\nL70@P1: Sarathi-Serve\nL71@P1: (b) High tail latency.\nL72@P1: Figure 1: Yi-34B running on two A100 GPUs serving 128\nL73@P1: requests from arxiv-summarisation trace. 1a highlights one\nL74@P1: of the many generation stalls lasting over several seconds in\nL75@P1: vLLM [53]. 1b shows the impact of increasing load on tail\nL76@P1: latency. Sarathi-Serve improves throughput while eliminating\nL77@P1: generation stalls.\nL78@P1: 1 Introduction\nL79@P1: Large language models (LLMs) [34,35,52,57,71] have shown\nL80@P1: impressive abilities in a wide variety of tasks spanning nat\u0002ural language processing, question answering, code gener\u0002ation, etc. This has led to tremendous increase in their us\u0002age across many applications such as chatbots [2, 5, 6, 57],\nL81@P1: search [4, 9, 11, 19, 25], code assistants [1, 8, 20], etc. The\nL82@P1: significant GPU compute required for running inference on\nL83@P1: large models, coupled with significant increase in their usage,\nL84@P1: has made LLM inference a dominant GPU workload today.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFAQ - Ollama (https://docs.ollama.com/faq)\nciteturn57view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn56view1\",\"pattern\":\"OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL\"}); Total lines: 473\nL339: ##\nL340: \nL341: cite39†​ L342: \nL343: How do I manage the maximum number of requests the Ollama server can queue?\nL344: \nL345: If too many requests are sent to the server, it will respond with a 503 error indicating the server is overloaded. You can adjust how many requests may be queued by setting `OLLAMA_MAX_QUEUE`.\nL346: ##\nL347: \nL348: cite40†​ L349: \nL350: How does Ollama handle concurrent requests?\nL351: Ollama supports two levels of concurrent processing. If your system has sufficient available memory (system memory when using CPU inference, or VRAM for GPU inference) then multiple models can be loaded at the same time. For a given model, if there is sufficient available memory when the model is loaded, it is configured to allow parallel request processing.\nL352: If there is insufficient available memory to load a new model request while one or more models are already loaded, all new requests will be queued until the new model can be loaded. As prior models become idle, one or more will be unloaded to make room for the new model. Queued requests will be processed in order. When using GPU inference new models must be able to completely fit in VRAM to allow concurrent model loads.\nL353: Parallel request processing for a given model results in increasing the context size by the number of parallel requests. For example, a 2K context with 4 parallel requests will result in an 8K context and additional memory allocation. The following server settings may be used to adjust how Ollama handles concurrent requests on most platforms:\nL354:   * `OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS` - The maximum number of models that can be loaded concurrently provided they fit in available memory. The default is 3 * the number of GPUs or 3 for CPU inference.\nL355:   * `OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL` - The maximum number of parallel requests each model will process at the same time, default 1. Required RAM will scale by `OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL` * `OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH`.\nL356:   * `OLLAMA_MAX_QUEUE` - The maximum number of requests Ollama will queue when busy before rejecting additional requests. The default is 512\nL357: Note: Windows with Radeon GPUs currently default to 1 model maximum due to limitations in ROCm v5.7 for available VRAM reporting. Once ROCm v6.2 is available, Windows Radeon will follow the defaults above. You may enable concurrent model loads on Radeon on Windows, but ensure you don’t load more models than will fit into your GPU’s VRAM.\nL358: ##\nL359: \nL360: cite41†​ L361: \nL362: How does Ollama load models on multiple GPUs?\nL363: \nL364: When loading a new model, Ollama evaluates the required VRAM for the model against what is currently available. If the model will entirely fit on any single GPU, Ollama will load the model on that GPU. This typically provides the best performance as it reduces the amount of data transferring across the PCI bus during inference. If the model does not fit entirely on one GPU, then it will be spread across all the available GPUs.\nL365: ##\nL366: \nL367: cite42†​ L368: \nL369: How can I enable Flash Attention?\nL370: \nL371: Flash Attention is a feature of most modern models that can significantly reduce memory usage as the context size grows. Ollama uses Flash Attention automatically when the selected backend and devices support it. To force Flash Attention on, set `OLLAMA_FLASH_ATTENTION=1` when starting the Ollama server. To disable it, set `OLLAMA_FLASH_ATTENTION=0`.\nL372: ##\nL373: \nL374: cite43†​ L375: \nL376: How can I set the quantization type for the K/V cache?\nL377: \nL378: The K/V context cache can be quantized to significantly reduce memory usage when Flash Attention is enabled. To use quantized K/V cache with Ollama you can set the following environment variable:\nL379: \nL380:   * `OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE` - The quantization type for the K/V cache. Default is `f16`.\nL381: \nL382: Currently this is a global option - meaning all models will run with the specified quantization type.\nL383: The currently available K/V cache quantization types are:\nL384:   * `f16` - high precision and memory usage (default).\nL385:   * `q8_0` - 8-bit quantization, uses approximately 1/2 the memory of `f16` with a very small loss in precision, this usually has no noticeable impact on the model’s quality (recommended if not using f16).\nL386:   * `q4_0` - 4-bit quantization, uses approximately 1/4 the memory of `f16` with a small-medium loss in precision that may be more noticeable at higher context sizes.\nL387: How much the cache quantization impacts the model’s response quality will depend on the model and the task. Models that have a high GQA count (e.g. Qwen2) may see a larger impact on precision from quantization than models with a low GQA count. You may need to experiment with different quantization types to find the best balance between memory usage and quality.\nL388: ##\nL389: \nL390: cite44†​ L391: \nL392: Where can I find my Ollama Public Key?\nL393: \nL394: Your Ollama Public Key is the public part of the key pair that lets your local Ollama instance talk to cite2†ollama.com†ollama.com . You’ll need it to:\nL395: \nL396:   * Push models to Ollama\nL397:   * Pull private models from Ollama to your machine\nL398:   * Run models hosted in cite45†Ollama Cloud†ollama.com L399: ###\nL400: \nL401: cite46†​ L402: \nL403: How to Add the Key\nL404: \nL405:   * Sign-in via the Settings page in the Mac and Windows App\nL406:   * Sign‑in via CLI\nL407: \nL408:     `ollama signin\nL409:     `\nL410: \nL411:   * Manually copy & paste the key on the Ollama Keys page: cite47†https://ollama.com/settings/keys†ollama.com L412: ###\nL413: \nL414: cite48†​ L415: \nL416: Where the Ollama Public Key lives\nL417: \nL418: OS  | Path to `id_ed25519.pub`\nL419: --- | ---\nL420: macOS  | `~/.ollama/id_ed25519.pub`\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRouteLLM: Learning to Route LLMs with Preference Data (https://arxiv.org/html/2406.18665v4)\nciteturn57view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn56view3\",\"pattern\":\"calibration\"}); Total lines: 406\nNo matching text found for \"calibration\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAbstentionBench: Reasoning LLMs Fail on Unanswerable Questions (https://arxiv.org/html/2506.09038v1)\nciteturn57view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn53view2\",\"pattern\":\"3 AbstentionBench\"}); Total lines: 787\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related work L18:   4. cite8†3 AbstentionBench: Benchmarking LLM Abstention L19:     1. cite9†3.1 Systematically collecting AbstentionBench datasets L20:     2. cite10†3.2 Grouping AbstentionBench datasets into scenarios L21:     3. cite11†3.3 Frontier LLMs L22:     4. cite12†3.4 Automatic abstention evaluation with an LLM-as-Judge L23:   5. cite13†4 Experiments L24:     1. cite14†4.1 Abstention is an open challenge for language models L25:     2. cite15†4.2 Post-training instills select abstention capabilities L26:     3. cite16†4.3 Reasoning degrades abstention L27:     4. cite17†4.4 Crafting a system prompt can boost abstention L28:   6. cite18†5 Discussion L29:   7. 