ite57†Underspecified Context (BBQ) L68:       5. cite58†False Premise (CoCoNot/False presumptions) L69:       6. cite59†Subjective (CoCoNot/Humanizing) L70:     3. cite60†F.3 Failure to abstain on reasoning datasets from s1.1 32B L71:       1. cite61†MMLU-Math-Abstain L72:     4. cite62†F.4 GPQA-Diamond-Abstain L73:     5. cite63†F.5 GSM8K-Abstain L74:       1. cite64†UMWP L75: cite65†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L76: \nL77: arXiv:2506.09038v1 [cs.AI] 10 Jun 2025\nL78: # AbstentionBench: Reasoning LLMs Fail on Unanswerable Questions\nL79: \nL80: Polina Kirichenko Affiliation: FAIR at Meta Joint co-first author; author order determined by random shuffling.    Mark Ibrahim Affiliation: FAIR at Meta Joint co-first author; author order determined by random shuffling.    Kamalika Chaudhuri Affiliation: FAIR at Meta    Samuel J. Bell Affiliation: FAIR at Meta Joint co-first author; author order determined by random shuffling.\nL81: ###### Abstract\nL82: For Large Language Models (LLMs) to be reliably deployed in both everyday and high-stakes domains, knowing when not to answer is equally critical as answering correctly. Real-world user queries, which can be underspecified, ill-posed, or fundamentally unanswerable, require LLMs to reason about uncertainty and selectively abstain—i.e., refuse to answer definitively. However, abstention remains understudied, without a systematic evaluation framework for modern LLMs.\nL83: In this work, we introduce AbstentionBench: a large-scale benchmark for holistically evaluating abstention across 20 diverse datasets, including questions with unknown answers, underspecification, false premises, subjective interpretations, and outdated information. Evaluating 20 frontier LLMs reveals abstention is an unsolved problem, and one where scaling models is of little use.\nL117: Yet, the effects of reasoning beyond correctness are not well understood, particularly for reasoning about uncertainty (cite109†Yin et al., 2024 ). Here, we take a step towards understanding the effect of reasoning fine-tuning on handling uncertainty.\nL118: Unanswerable math problems.\nL119: Despite impressive progress in mathematical reasoning in LLMs (cite108†Luo et al., 2025 ; cite110†Lewkowycz et al., 2022 ; cite81†Muennighoff et al., 2025 ; cite80†DeepSeek-AI, 2025 ; cite111†Yang et al., 2024 ; cite112†Shao et al., 2024 ; cite113†Yu et al., 2023 ), most evaluations have focused on answerable math problems (cite114†Hendrycks et al., 2021 ; cite74†Cobbe et al., 2021 ; cite115†Lightman et al., 2023 ; cite76†Hendrycks et al., 2020 ; cite116†Glazer et al., 2024 ; cite117†Sun et al., 2025 ; cite118†Balunović et al., 2025 ).\nL120: Emerging research is investigating how LLMs respond to unanswerable or unsolvable math problems, which probes at their capabilities to robustly reason about claims and evidence. cite119†Ma et al. (2025) and cite120†Rahman et al. (2025) construct synthetic LLM-generated datasets with unsolvable math problems by prompting LLMs with examples from standard math benchmarks. cite121†Shi et al.\nL121: (2023) evaluate how easily LLMs get distracted by irrelevant context in math problems, while cite122†Ouyang (2025) generate unsolvable problems by pruning necessary conditions from tree-structured math problems. cite123†Zhou et al. (2024) evaluate robustness of LLMs on math problems, including perturbations which make the problems unanswerable. cite124†Saadat et al. (2024) also evaluate LLMs on the UMWP dataset (cite125†Sun et al., 2024 ) which is used in AbstentionBench.\nL122: While these works present initial evaluations of LLMs on unsolvable math, it is not well understood how reasoning-finetuned models handle unanswerable math problems, which we study in depth in our work.\nL123: ## 3 AbstentionBench: Benchmarking LLM Abstention\nL124: We now introduce AbstentionBench, a large-scale and challenging benchmark for evaluating LLM abstention ability across diverse scenarios. Across a range of tasks, AbstentionBench covers cases where models should and should not abstain. We define abstention as a response that refrains from directly answering the question, such as by expressing a lack of knowledge, communicating uncertainty or caveats, or highlighting unanswerable aspects of the prompt.\nL125: This can include simple statements such as “I don’t know” or “I can’t answer”, but can also include detailed responses providing partial answers to only certain aspects of the prompt.\nL126: ### 3.1 Systematically collecting AbstentionBench datasets\nL127: \nL128: To source a challenging mix of datasets, we began with a systematic search of existing datasets relating to abstention, refusal, and uncertainty, producing a shortlist of 82 datasets. Each shortlisted dataset was reviewed in depth by the authors, retaining only those where abstention is a desirable model response for at least some samples.\nL129: General domain datasets.\nL130: This resulted in the following 16 datasets included in AbstentionBench from a diverse set of domains: ALCUNA (cite126†Yin et al., 2023b ); Bias Benchmark for Question Answering (BBQ) (cite127†Parrish et al., 2022 ); the ‘Disambiguate’ and ‘Known Unknowns’ tasks from BIG-Bench (BB) (cite128†Srivastava et al., 2023 ); CoCoNot (CCN) (cite95†Brahman et al., 2024 ); FalseQA (cite129†Hu et al., 2023 ); FreshQA (cite130†Vu et al., 2023 ); Known Unknown Questions (KUQ) (cite84†Amayuelas et al., 2024 ); MediQ (cite131†Li et al., 2024 ); MoralChoice (cite132†Scherrer et al., 2023 ); Musique (cite85†Slobodkin et al., 2023 ); (QA)^{2} (cite133†Kim et al., 2023 ); QASPER (cite134†Dasigi et al., 2021 ); the ‘Geo’ subset of SituatedQA (cite135†Zhang and Choi, 2021 ); SQuAD 2.0 (cite136†Rajpurkar et al., 2018 ); and WorldSense (cite137†Benchekroun et al., 2023 ).\nL131: We consider FreshQA questions unanswerable if the correct answer has changed since the most recent model knowledge cut-off. CCN and KUQ were partitioned into subsets by question type, with some irrelevant subsets removed. Datasets span various tasks and domains, from web search queries to medical question answering, moral dilemma to geographic knowledge.\nL132: Math and science datasets. To facilitate our analysis of abstention on reasoning-heavy domains, we incorporate additional math and science datasets. We first modify three datasets—GPQA-Diamond (cite75†Rein et al., 2024 ); GSM8K (cite74†Cobbe et al., 2021 ); and the ‘college mathematics’, ‘abstract algebra’, and ‘high school mathematics’ subsets of MMLU (cite76†Hendrycks et al., 2020 ) which we refer to as MMLU-Math—such that they contain a mix of answerable and unanswerable questions.\nL133: To create the unanswerable questions, we first filter for problems which contain context before the final question. Then we duplicate the original answerable questions before removing all context up until the start of the question, thus removing key information required to answer appropriately. We refer to these datasets as GPQA-Abstain, GSM8K-Abstain, and MMLU-Math-Abstain.\nL134: To these, we also add Unanswerable Math Word Problems (UMWP) (cite125†Sun et al., 2024 ) with questions drawn from other math datasets and modified to be unanswerable.\nL135: See cite22†appendix C for full details of dataset search, selection criteria, and implementation details for all datasets, and cite45†appendix F for qualitative examples.\nL136: ### 3.2 Grouping AbstentionBench datasets into scenarios\nL137: \nL138: Abstention is a desirable response under many scenarios. By analyzing the datasets described in the previous section, we identified six key scenarios where models should abstain, which we use for grouping our results and highlighting trends. These scenarios are neither exhaustive nor mutually exclusive, but do give an indication of the breadth of abstention requirements. See cite22†appendix C to see each dataset’s scenario.\n"
